# DESCRIPTION

devmgr finds the machine's devices and runs a driver for each one that a driver claims.
It is a service, started by launcher(8) from a manifest (manifest(6)) that gives it the machine's memory-mapped, interrupt and, on amd64, I/O port resources, on arm64 its SMC functions, the system resource, the firmware's boot data, and `programs`, bootfs and a job to run the drivers in.

It works in this order.

- It reads the firmware's tables from the boot data and loads the ACPI namespace: the DSDT and SSDTs, run with an interpreter whose regions lie over the machine, and `_INI`.
  It tells the firmware that the interrupt controller is the APIC.
  It reads `_STA` of every device and, of those present, the hardware id, compatible ids and `_CRS`.
- On a machine whose firmware gave only a devicetree, it reads the tree the boot data carries, the one the loader made the ACPI tables from (intro(8)).
  A device is an enabled node with a `compatible` on the root or on a `simple-bus`, or with a `compatible` and a `reg` under an I2C controller (a node named `i2c` or `i2c@<address>`), whose `reg` is its address on that bus and which links to the controller (`i2c`, below); the children of any other node are its driver's.
  Its registers are its `reg` ranges, translated through each bus's `ranges` to the addresses the CPU reaches them by.
  Its interrupts, `interrupts` on its `interrupt-parent` or `interrupts-extended`, are followed through each `interrupt-map` to the kernel's interrupt controller, a PLIC, an APLIC or a GIC, and given by its number there.
  The kernel's interrupt controllers and timers are not devices.
- It finds the PCI functions by walking the configuration space of segment 0 through the MCFG table's ECAM windows, from each window's first bus (a machine can have a host bridge per window, as the CIX Sky1's three do).
  A vendor's PCIe host, such as the SpacemiT K1's (k1-pcie(3)), is a devicetree device whose driver brings its bus up; once the other devices' drivers are started, devmgr reads each such host's `pci` (its driver's class is `pci`) and binds the functions it lists the same way, in the host's segment.
- It names each IOMMU the DMAR, IVRS, IORT or RIMT tables describe to the kernel.
- It reads the drivers' match records, `etc/drivers` in bootfs (driver(6)), and matches every PCI function, every present ACPI device and every device of the devicetree against them.
  A device with no match is `unbound`; one with two drivers tied at the deciding level is `ambiguous`, and neither runs.
- For each bound PCI function it turns on decoding of the function's memory and I/O ranges.
  Bus mastering is left to the driver.
- It runs each bound device's driver as a service of its own, called `<driver>-<device>` (`edu-00:10.0`), with the manifest `export` and `restart on-failure`, so that a driver that fails is restarted.
  The device is named `pci-<bus>:<device>.<function>`, `acpi-` and its namespace path without the leading backslash, or `dt-` and its node's name (`dt-ethernet@cac80000`; where two devices' nodes share a name, their paths' components joined by dots).
  Drivers whose class is `power` start first.

A driver's process is given its device's resources and nothing else.
A PCI function's driver gets a resource for each memory or I/O range of its BARs, one for its 4 KiB of configuration space, the interrupt its pin is routed to by `_PRT` (for a function on the root bus), a single MSI-X or MSI vector if the function has the capability and the kernel can make one, and a bus transaction initiator (BTI) for its DMA on the IOMMU that covers the function, narrowed to the driver's `dma-bits`.
A memory BAR's resource is at the address the CPU reaches it by: the BAR's bus address plus the translation of the root bridge window that decodes it, from the bridge's `_CRS` (`PNP0A08` or `PNP0A03`); a BAR that no window of its bridge decodes is left out.
A function a host driver gives has its configuration space where the host fixed it, the host's memory windows as its bridge's, DMA narrowed to what the host reaches as well as to `dma-bits`, and its MSI vector from the host's MSI block, which devmgr gives the kernel as an MSI frame (msi(2)) from the host's description: its registers, its target and the interrupt it raises.
Where no IOMMU covers it, the BTI is the stub's, which lets the device reach all of memory, and `status` says so.
An ACPI device's driver gets the memory and I/O ranges and the first interrupt its `_CRS` names, and a BTI: on the SMMUv3 where the IORT names it as a named component (its device object's path matched segment by segment), which translates every stream id the IORT gives it, and the stub's otherwise; where its `_CCA` says its DMA isn't cache coherent, the driver is started with the argument `--noncoherent`, and maps the memory it shares with the device uncached.
A devicetree device's driver gets a memory resource for each of its `reg` ranges, six at most, the first of its interrupts the kernel's controller takes, and the stub's BTI; where the nearest `dma-noncoherent` or `dma-coherent`, on its node or a bus above it, says its DMA isn't cache coherent (on RISC-V it is unless the tree says otherwise, on arm64 it isn't), the driver is started with `--noncoherent`.
Its BTI reaches only as far as the buses above it map addresses unchanged: for each bus whose `dma-ranges` has a range from address 0 to the CPU's address 0, that range's size rounded down to a power of two (the SpacemiT K1's storage bus's 2 GiB: 31 bits), or less if the driver's record says so.
A devicetree device's links are what its node's phandles name: each `clocks`, `resets`, `power-domains` and `phys` specifier, with its cells and its name from the matching `clock-names`, `reset-names`, `power-domain-names` or `phy-names`; each `usb-phy`, the older USB binding's PHY, a `phy` link named `usb-phy`; each `pinctrl-<n>` state, named by `pinctrl-names`; each `<name>-supply`; each `<name>-gpios` or `gpios` specifier; each interrupt on a controller other than the kernel's (a GPIO block's); each `spacemit,apmu`, a K1 device's control registers in the APMU, a `syscon` link whose cell is their offset; and each `spacemit,apbc`, the K1 APBC a device needs (the pin controller its keys), a `syscon` link with no cells; and for a device on an I2C bus, an `i2c` link to its controller, whose cell is its address, through whose `bus` (k1-i2c(3)) its driver reaches it.
A PCI host's root ports' links (their `phys` and slots' supplies) are the host's, and a binding may imply a link no phandle names: each of the K1's PCIe PHYs links to the combo PHY, `phy calibration`, whose calibration it takes (k1-pcie-phy(3)).
A link's provider is the device whose node is the one named or holds it (a pin controller holds its states, an I2C controller its PMIC's regulators); a fixed clock outside any bus is no device's.
A provider's driver is started before the drivers of the devices that link to it, and its tree is given to each of them at `/dev/provider/<service>`; each devicetree device's driver is given its directory under `platform` (below) at `/dev/dt`, whose `links` says which of its links each provider serves and the cells that say which clock or reset is its own.
CIX's `CLKT` and `RSTL` fill the same record for an ACPI device: a clock's cell its SCMI id, a reset's its index on its controller.
After its own ranges an ACPI device's driver gets those of the devices firmware links it to: the devices its `_DSD` device properties name as `shmem` and `mboxes` (an SCMI agent's shared memory and mailboxes) and as `dp_phy` (a DisplayPort transmitter's PHY: a child of the PHY's device names the port, and the PHY's registers are that device's), then the reset controllers its `RSTL` names (CIX's table of a device's resets).
The driver is told which is which by an argument for each, `--bar=<index>:<role>`, the role `shmem`, `mboxes`, `phy`, `reset` or `firmware`.
A board rule adds what firmware's tables leave out: on the CIX Sky1 (FADT `CIXTEK` `SKY1EDK2`) its SCMI agent, `CIXHA006`, also gets an SMC resource for SiP function `0xc2000001` and TF-A's shared memory at `0x84380000`, as its `firmware` range; devmgr logs `firmware smc c2000001`, or why the kernel refused.
Each ACPI device's driver is given its directory under `platform` (below) at `/dev/acpi`, and one whose device has clocks (`CLKT`) or SCMI power domains (`_DSD`'s `power-domains`) the tree of the running `power` driver, the SCMI agent (scmi(3)), at `/dev/scmi`.
A display engine and its DisplayPort transmitter are paired, the n-th display-class device with the n-th link-class one by their first register range's base: the transmitter's driver (dptx(3)) is given the engine's platform directory at `/dev/engine`, to read its power before any register, and is started before the display drivers, whose engine's gets the transmitter's tree at `/dev/link`; devmgr mounts each transmitter's tree in its own at `dev/link<n>`, beside `dev/display<n>`.
A power-class driver whose tree has an `rtc`, a real-time clock (p1(3)), is mounted at `dev/rtc<n>` too, the first `dev/rtc0`, the time service's (time(4)).
A driver whose class is `input` or `usb` is also given a channel to kbdfs(4)'s `kbin`, at `/dev/kbin`, and one to mouse(4)'s `mousein`, at `/dev/mousein`.
A driver whose class is `net` serves an `ether` directory in its tree (ether(3)), which devmgr binds in its `net` directory as `ether0`, `ether1` and so on, in the order the drivers start.
A driver whose class is `block` and whose records name an `sd` letter serves an `sd` directory (sd(3)), which devmgr binds in its `dev` directory as `sd`<letter>`0`, `1` and so on, each letter counted apart, in the order the drivers start; one that fails to start its disk serves none, and takes no number.

devmgr serves its tree at once, before it has finished, so that `status` grows as it goes and ends with `bound <n>` when it is done.
Each driver's own tree is mounted under `drivers`.

The manifest:

```text
service devmgr
program devmgr
resource mmio
resource irq
resource system
resource ioport
resource smc
bootdata
mount /dev/kbin kbdfs/kbin
mount /dev/mousein mouse/mousein
programs
export
```

`resource ioport` is there on amd64 only, and `resource smc` on arm64 only.
A manifest that uses devmgr typically mounts its `dev` at `/dev`, after kbdfs's and the mouse's, and its `net` at `/net`.

Without an MCFG table there is no PCI, and devmgr says so in the log, `devmgr: no MCFG: no PCI`.
A machine devmgr cannot use stops it with a message: no ACPI tables in the boot data, a driver index in error (the file and line are given), or no bootfs or job.

# EXAMPLES

The devices and what became of them:

```rc
% cat /svc/devmgr/status
```

# FILES

: `/svc/devmgr/status`
  A line for each device, ACPI's and PCI's: the device's name, its ids, its class, the driver service, its state, `dma` and where its DMA goes, such as `pci-00:10.0 1234:11e8 00.ff.00 edu-00:10.0 running dma <where>`.
  The ids are the PCI vendor and device in hexadecimal, or the ACPI hardware id or `-`.
  The class is the PCI class, subclass and programming interface, or `-`.
  The driver service is `-` where there is none.
  The state is `unbound`, `ambiguous: ` and the drivers tied, `not started`, `running` or `failed`.
  DMA is `-` before a device is given its resources, `none` where it has no BTI, the IOMMU's name (`VT-d fed90000`) where it has one, `passthrough: no IOMMU covers it` for the stub, `translated by` and the IOMMU's name for a named component, or the IOMMU's name and `refused it:` and why (a named component refused then gets the stub's, `; passthrough`).
  When devmgr has finished, the last line is `bound <n>`, the number of drivers running.
: `/svc/devmgr/iommus`
  A line for each IOMMU unit, `<name> ok` or `<name> refused <n>`; `no system resource: no DMA`, and `no IOMMU: DMA is passthrough (WARNING)` where there is none.
  `not yet` until devmgr gets there.
: `/svc/devmgr/enumeration`
  How many PCI functions it found, in how many microseconds, and the buses it mapped.
  `not yet` until then.
: `/svc/devmgr/acpi`
  The tables read, the time to load the namespace, run `_INI` and `_STA`, `_PIC` and `_PRT`, with the failures, `\_S5` and its sleep types (or `no \_S5`), each PCI root bridge's buses and memory windows (`root bridge buses 96-127 0x40000000-0x4fffffff`, a window's translation after it as `+0x...`), and the pages of physical memory mapped.
  `not yet` until then.
: `/svc/devmgr/devicetree`
  A line for each device of the devicetree: its path, its compatibles joined by commas, `mmio <base>+<size>` for each range, `irq <number> <trigger>` for each interrupt (`level-high`, `level-low`, `edge-rising` or `edge-falling`), `noncoherent` where its DMA isn't, `dma-bits <n>` where its buses narrow its DMA, and `iommu <unit's path>` and its id for each `iommus` entry, as in `/soc/network-bus/ethernet@cac80000 spacemit,k1-emac mmio 0xcac80000+0x420 irq 131 level-high noncoherent dma-bits 31`.
  Below it, indented, a line for each link, `<kind> <name> <provider's path> [<node's path>] <cells>`, the kind `clock`, `reset`, `power-domain`, `phy`, `pinctrl`, `supply`, `gpio` or `interrupt`, `-` for no name or no provider, and the node named where it isn't the provider's own, as in `clock core /soc/system-controller@d4282800 10`; then what couldn't be resolved: `reg <n> not translated`, `interrupt <n> unresolved`, or a phandle that names no node.
  `no devicetree` on a machine whose boot data has none, and `not yet` until devmgr gets there.
: `/svc/devmgr/cpus`
  A line for each processor device, from its `_CPC` and `_PSD`, evaluated when read, for power(8): `<uid> <path> perf <lowest> <lowest nonlinear> <nominal> <highest> mhz <lowest MHz> <nominal MHz> desired <space> <address> <bits> <bit offset> domain <domain> <coordination> <processors>`.
  `desired none` where `_CPC` names no desired-performance register (or the null one), and no `domain` where there is no `_PSD`; `<uid> <path> no _CPC` for a processor without one, or whose levels aren't numbers.
  Empty before ACPI's namespace has loaded, and on a machine without one.
: `/svc/devmgr/thermal`
  A line for each thermal zone, its methods evaluated when read: `<path> <_TMP> passive <_PSV> critical <_CRT> polling <_TZP>`, temperatures in tenths of a kelvin and polling in tenths of a second, `-` for a method it lacks or a temperature that failed (0 or Ones), and polling `0` without `_TZP`.
: `/svc/devmgr/dev`
  A directory for the devices' classes, to mount at `/dev`: each disk's sd(3) directory, `sdF0` and on.
  It holds `reboot`, which takes a command a line, `reboot`, `poweroff` or `warmboot`, through the power resource devmgr makes from its system resource, and refuses anything else (`poweroff` goes first to the `ctl` of a running driver whose manifest says `poweroff`, driver(6)), and `warm`, whose `kernel`, `bootfs` and `cmdline` hold what a warm boot takes, each as long as its last write; before a warm boot devmgr turns bus mastering off on every PCI function it found through ECAM, ends the drivers of the functions a host driver gave, then each running devicetree device's driver (the hosts' among them), whatever its restart policy, then asserts the resets its links name (its providers' `ctl`, `reset <id> assert`; a driver first, since on the K1 a read of a block held in reset stalls the CPU that makes it), and if the kernel refuses turns bus mastering back on, releases the resets and starts those drivers again (reboot(8)).
: `/svc/devmgr/net`
  The Ethernet devices, `ether0` and on, each a driver's ether(3) directory; to mount at `/net`.
: `/svc/devmgr/drivers`
  A directory of the drivers' own trees, one for each driver service that is running: `drivers/edu-00:10.0/status`.
: `/svc/devmgr/platform`
  A directory for each bound ACPI device's driver service, the driver's `/dev/acpi`, and for each bound devicetree device's, the driver's `/dev/dt`.
  A devicetree device's has `compatible`, its node's compatibles a line each, and `links`, a line for each link as `devicetree` gives it but with the provider named by its driver's service, mounted in the driver at `/dev/provider/<service>`, or `-` where no driver runs it: `clock core k1-syscon-dt-system-controller@d4282800 10`.
  `node` has the properties of the device's node and of the nodes under it, a line each, `<name> <value>` with the value as dtc writes it (strings quoted, cells in angle brackets, other bytes in square ones, nothing for an empty one), a property of a node under it named by that node's path below the device's: `phy-mode "rgmii-id"`, `mdio-bus/phy@1/reg <0x1>`.
  `clocks` lists the device's clocks, `<SCMI id> <name>` a line, and `resets` its resets, `<controller's path> <index> <name>`, as CIX's `CLKT` and `RSTL` give them; both are empty on other firmware.
  A device with power resources, in its own `_PR0` or that of the device its `_DSD` `power-supply` names, also has `power`: writing `on` runs the `_ON` of each resource devmgr hasn't turned on yet, whatever its `_STA` says (firmware can set its bit with the rest of `_ON` undone, as the CIX Sky1 does for its GPU's), then the device's `_PS0`; `off` runs `_PS3`, then the `_OFF` of each resource devmgr turned on.
  A write fails if a method failed; reading gives the last state written, and before any, `on` if every one of those resources' `_STA` says it is on, as firmware left it, else `off`: a driver asks before it reads a device firmware may have left lit, as linlon(3) does.
  devmgr logs what it ran, `devmgr: <service>: power on: \_SB_.GPUP.PPRS._ON`.

