# DIAGNOSTICS

devmgr prints to the log what it found, `devmgr: <n> PCI functions, <n> ACPI devices, <n> devicetree devices, <n> IOMMUs; <n> to bind`, and `devmgr: bound <n>` when it is done, and why any driver could not be started.
An error that stops it begins `devmgr:`.

# BUGS

Only PCI segment 0 is read through ECAM; another segment is a host driver's.
A host driver's bus is read once: a device it finds later isn't bound.
A function's interrupt is routed only if it is on the root bus; one behind a bridge has none.
A function gets one MSI vector.
An ACPI device gets six ranges at most, its own and its linked ones together.
A devicetree device's links reach its providers only through their drivers' trees: no driver yet serves power domains or a GPIO block's interrupts, and a provider held by another device (a PMIC behind an I2C controller) is that device's, whose driver doesn't serve it.
An ACPI device's links aren't given as `/dev/provider`: its clocks are the SCMI agent's, at `/dev/scmi`, and its reset controllers' registers are its own ranges.
The units a devicetree's `iommus` names are not given to the kernel, so a device behind one has the stub's BTI.
On a devicetree machine there is no ECAM: the loader makes no MCFG, even for a plain ECAM host, so PCI is only what a host driver gives.
A power resource another device also names is turned off with either; nothing counts its users.

The IOMMUs: VT-d devices behind bridges, AMD-Vi exclusion ranges and SMMUv1 and v2 are not handled, and a device they would cover has no BTI on an IOMMU.
